## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple target nodes efficiently?
- How does the solution change if edges can have zero weight, or if counts exceed 64-bit, and you need a different modulus or exact arithmetic?

Why This Is Asked\n\nThis question probes understanding of practical sorting trade-offs beyond textbook asymptotics, including cache locality, in-place constraints, and robust pivot strategies. It invites discussion of 3-way partitioning to handle duplicates, median-of-three to curb worst-case behavior, and a small insertion sort for tiny partitions.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- 3-way partitioning in quicksort\n- median-of-three pivot selection\n- insertion sort threshold tuning\n- in-place algorithms and cache efficiency\n- iterative vs recursive implementations\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
function sortInPlace(arr){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(a,i,j)=>{const t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  const medianOfThree=(a,i,j,k)=>{let x=[a[i],i],[a[j],j],[a[k],k].sort((p,q)=>p[0]-q[0]); return a[x[1]];};
  // iterative 3-way quicksort skeleton
  const stack=[[0,arr.length-1]];
  while(stack.length){
    const [lo,hi]=stack.pop();
    if(hi-lo+1<=TH){ // insertion sort for small partitions
      for(let i=lo+1;i<=hi;i++){
        let t=arr[i], j=i-1;
        while(j>=lo && arr[j]>t){ arr[j+1]=arr[j]; j-- }
        arr[j+1]=t;
      }
      continue;
    }
    // median-of-three pivot
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
    const pivot= medianOfThree(arr, lo, mid, hi);
    // 3-way partition
    let lt=lo, i=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(arr[i]<pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
      else if(arr[i]>pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
      else i++;
    }
    stack.push([lo, lt-1]);
    stack.push([gt+1, hi]);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP understanding with obstacles and modular arithmetic; ensures edge-case handling and ability to implement both bottom-up and top-down approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with blockers (Fibonacci variant)
- Bottom-up vs top-down memoization
- Space optimization (O(1) possible with two variables)
- Modulo handling with BigInt or large integers

## Edge Cases
- **Start blocked (step 0)**: Return 0 immediately — no valid paths exist
- **End blocked (step N)**: dp[N] remains 0 since we cannot land on N
- **Consecutive blocked steps**: Creates a barrier; all steps after a chain of 2+ blocked steps will have dp[i]=0 (cannot be reached)

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1000000007n;

// Bottom-up DP: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysBottomUp(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0n);
  dp[0] = 1n;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) {
      dp[i] = 0n; // blocked step: no ways to land here
      continue;
    }
    dp[i] = (dp[i-1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i-2] : 0n)) % MOD;
  }
  return Number(dp[n]);
}

// Top-down memoization: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysTopDown(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const memo = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1n);

  function solve(i) {
    if (i < 0) return 0n;    if (i === 0) return 1n;
    if (blocked[i]) return 0n; // this step is blocked
    if (memo[i] !== -1n) return memo[i];

    memo[i] = (solve(i-1) + solve(i-2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }

  return Number(solve(n));
}

// Space-optimized: O(N) time, O(1) space
function countWaysOptimized(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let prev2 = 1n, prev1 = blocked[1] ? 0n : 1n;

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0n : (prev1 + prev2) % MOD;
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = curr;
  }

  return Number(n === 0 ? prev2 : prev1);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time**: O(N) for all variants — each step computed once
- **Space**: O(N) for basic DP/memoization, O(1) for optimized version
- **Modulo**: Applied at each addition to prevent overflow

## Why This Is Asked

Tests algorithmic depth and practical handling of worst-case quicksort behavior.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth guard
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid pathological inputs
- In-place partition and recursion depth management
- Complexity: O(n log n) average and worst-case; O(log n) stack space

## Code Example

```javascript
// Introsort skeleton in JS
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  if (n <= 1) return;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n));
  function swap(i, j) { const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t; }
  function medianOfThree(lo, mid, hi) {
    if (arr[lo] > arr[mid]) swap(lo, mid);
    if (arr[lo] > arr[hi]) swap(lo, hi);
    if (arr[mid] > arr[hi]) swap(mid, hi);
    return mid;
  }
  function partition(lo, hi) {
    const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
    const m = medianOfThree(lo, mid, hi);
    swap(m, hi);
    const pivot = arr[hi];
    let i = lo;
    for (let j = lo; j < hi; j++) {
      if (arr[j] <= pivot) { swap(i, j); i++; }
    }
    swap(i, hi);
    return i;
  }
  function heapSort(lo, hi) {
    const n = hi - lo + 1;
    function heapify(i, size) {
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let largest = i;
        if (l < size && arr[lo + l] > arr[lo + largest]) largest = l;
        if (r < size && arr[lo + r] > arr[lo + largest]) largest = r;
        if (largest !== i) { swap(lo + i, lo + largest); i = largest; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(n / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, n);
    for (let end = n - 1; end > 0; end--) {
      swap(lo, lo + end);
      heapify(0, end);
    }
  }
  function insertionSort(lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i <= hi; i++) {
      const key = arr[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && arr[j] > key) { arr[j + 1] = arr[j]; j--; }
      arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function quickSort(lo, hi, depth) {
    const size = hi - lo + 1;
    if (size <= 16) { insertionSort(lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth <= 0) { heapSort(lo, hi); return; }
    const p = partition(lo, hi);
    quickSort(lo, p - 1, depth - 1);
    quickSort(p + 1, hi, depth - 1);
  }
  quickSort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to sort objects by a numeric key while preserving comparator locality?
- Compare cache behavior of introsort vs pure mergesort on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioned sequences with a non-additive batch cost and compares memoization vs tabulation. It also probes edge-case reasoning when K bounds vary.

## Key Concepts
- DP over contiguous partitions
- Range max tracking
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(a, K){
  const N=a.length, INF=1e18;
  const Kcap=Math.min(K,N);
  const dp=Array.from({length:N+1}, ()=>Array(Kcap+1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=N;i++){
    for(let k=1;k<=Math.min(i,Kcap);k++){
      let maxV=0;
      for(let j=i-1;j>=0;j--){
        maxV = Math.max(maxV, a[j]);
        dp[i][k] = Math.min(dp[i][k], dp[j][k-1] + maxV);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[N][Kcap];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you push to O(NK) with a monotone optimization?
- How would you adapt if batch cost were sum of maxes with a penalty per batch?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical Trie usage for autocomplete, encouraging explicit design choices and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Trie node with 26-child array and isWord flag
- DFS enumeration in lexicographic order
- Edge cases: empty string, duplicates, limit handling, memory usage

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() { this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.isWord = false; }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) p.next[i] = new TrieNode(); p = p.next[i]; } p.isWord = true; }
  search(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return p.isWord; }
  startsWith(pfx) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return true; }
  listWithPrefix(pfx, limit) { const res = []; let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return res; p = p.next[i]; } const dfs = (node, prefix) => { if (res.length >= limit) return; if (node.isWord) res.push(prefix); for (let c = 0; c < 26; c++) { const child = node.next[c]; if (child) dfs(child, prefix + String.fromCharCode(97+c)); } }; dfs(p, pfx); return res; }
}
```

